--TEST--
`Core\Db\Rows`'s readers answer the types spec § 18's Results table gives them, so a result set is read without a fetch-mode argument choosing the shape — `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members` and `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 18's Results table asserted as *types* rather than as values: nothing here
// reaches a server, and nothing needs to. A `Rows` member whose declared
// return type disagreed with that table would be a diagnostic at one of the
// six assignments below, so what this case checks is the whole row of it at
// once — which no single value could.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
var $rows = $db->query("select id, owner from accounts", []);

// `all` and `first` are written together because the pair is the claim:
// `fetchAll` and `fetch` differ in *type* here, and not in a mode flag passed
// to one member that returns whatever it was told to.
array<Core\Db\Row> $all = $rows->all();
?Core\Db\Row $first = $rows->first();

// `value` is `mixed`, because the first column of the first row is whatever
// `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`'s map made of that column.
mixed $value = $rows->value();

// `column` takes either half of its `int|string` key — a label or a position
// in the server's own description — and both answer the same array.
array<mixed> $byName = $rows->column("id");
array<mixed> $byPosition = $rows->column(0);

// `count` is a `uint` and it is exact: a `Rows` exists only after § 4's
// buffered read has already finished, so there is nothing left to estimate.
uint $count = $rows->count();

// And the bound on the other side, which is what makes `all` and `first` two
// members rather than one: an empty result is `null` and never an empty array,
// so the type says so at the assignment rather than at the first `foreach`.
array<Core\Db\Row> $wrong = $rows->first();
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `array<Core\Db\Row>`, found `null|Core\Db\Row`
   --> case.nvs:32:29
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
